course: dictionary
lesson: 13489
title: The igniter: what starts the fire
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An igniter is the part that starts a fire or makes a fuel catch light. Think of the clicker on a gas cooker: it makes the spark, and then the gas starts to burn.
explain: «The gas hob would not light, so Mum checked the igniter.» The clue is the word hob, which is the top of a cooker where you heat food. The igniter is the part that makes the gas light, so it must be the thing that starts the flame.
explain: Igniters are useful, but they can be dangerous. Only adults should use igniters and lighters. If you ever see one, do not touch it, and tell an adult.
fact: An igniter is a device or part that starts a fire or makes a fuel catch light.
fact: «The igniter clicked, and the gas lit with a blue flame.»
fact: In rockets and fireworks, a small charge called an igniter sets off the bigger burning.
fact: Igniters, lighters and matches are for adults to use only; never play with them.
fact: Igniter and ignitor are both spellings of the same word.
exercise: «The igniter clicked, and the gas lit with a blue flame.» What is the igniter? | The gas tank outside | The pan on the hob | The blue flame itself | *The part that starts the fire
exercise: Which of these uses an igniter to start burning? | A sofa | *A rocket motor | A bicycle bell | A pencil case
exercise: Ella asks, «Can I use the lighter to light the candles?» What is the safe answer? | Yes, because lighters are toys | Hide the lighter in your bag | Yes, if no one is looking | *Ask an adult to light them
test: «The igniter on the barbecue is broken, so the coals will not catch.» What does the igniter do? | *Starts the burning | Puts out the fire | Makes the food taste nice | Cools the grill
test: What is the safe rule about igniters and lighters? | *Only adults should use them; never play with them | Keep them in your pocket | Use them whenever you like | Let children play with them to learn
exam: What is the name for a small charge that sets off the bigger burning in a rocket or firework? | Cloud | *Igniter | Blanket | Ladder
